<description>
IPC clients can hold block templates indefinitely, keeping transactions alive after they leave the mempool. This adds `getMemoryLoad()` to estimate the memory held on behalf of all mining clients, as discussed in bitcoin/bitcoin#33899. It does not impose a limit.

The estimate includes each tracked template's vectors and the transactions it references that are no longer in the mempool. Transaction objects shared by multiple templates are counted once. Dummy coinbase transactions and the template cached by the `getblocktemplate` RPC are excluded.

`BlockTemplateManager` tracks templates through weak references, so the registry does not prolong their lifetime. Templates unregister when destroyed, including when IPC cleanup happens after shutdown has reset the manager. Clients can reduce the reported memory load by releasing stale templates or explicitly calling `destroy()`.

The result is an estimate: the mempool can change during calculation, and eviction followed by re-entry as a different transaction object can cause undercounting.

Example client usage: [stratum-mining/sv2-tp#63](https://github.com/stratum-mining/sv2-tp/pull/63).

Commits:

- `refactor: pass block template by reference to WaitAndCreateNewBlock`
- `refactor: share ownership of BlockTemplateManager`
- `mining: track block templates held by IPC clients`
- `mining: add GetTemplateMemoryUsage()`
- `ipc: add getMemoryLoad()`
- `test: check template memory accounting invariants`
</description>

<commits>
812958fdda refactor: pass block template by reference to WaitAndCreateNewBlock

It only reads the previous block hash and the fees, so it does not need
to know how the caller owns the template.

1fe62e7b07 refactor: share ownership of BlockTemplateManager

Prepare for a later commit where server-side templates hold weak
references to the manager. Locking these references during cleanup
temporarily extends the manager's lifetime.

2a0c57dc84 mining: track block templates held by IPC clients

IPC clients can hold on to block templates indefinitely, which has the
same impact as when the node holds a shared pointer to the
CBlockTemplate. Because each template in turn holds CTransactionRefs,
transactions that are removed from the mempool will not have their
memory freed.

Register each template handed out through the Mining interface with
BlockTemplateManager, as a weak_ptr so that the registry never extends
a template's lifetime. A later commit uses the registry to account for
the memory these templates keep alive.

Keep a weak reference to the original manager in each client template
and lock it when unregistering. IPC cleanup can run asynchronously
after shutdown resets the manager, so only unregister if it still
exists. UnregisterTemplate and the manager destructor only access
its own members, allowing cleanup to outlive its dependencies.

Co-authored-by: ismaelsadeeq <ask4ismailsadiq@gmail.com>

4b027e1a46 test: check template memory accounting invariants

Check exact memory usage through the mining interface with overlapping
templates from two clients.

Check template destruction after the manager is reset during shutdown.

ae765777b1 mining: add GetTemplateMemoryUsage()

Calculate the non-mempool memory footprint of the registered block
templates: their own vectors, plus the transactions they reference that
are no longer in the mempool. A transaction shared by several templates
is counted once.

Add bench logging to collect data on whether caching or simplified
heuristics are needed, such as not checking for mempool presence.

Co-authored-by: ismaelsadeeq <ask4ismailsadiq@gmail.com>

3718f47873 ipc: add getMemoryLoad()

Allow IPC clients to inspect the amount of memory consumed by the block
templates they hold, including transactions that are no longer in the
mempool.

Returns a MemoryLoad struct which can later be expanded to e.g.
include a limit.

Expand the interface_ipc_mining.py test to demonstrate the behavior and
to illustrate how clients can call destroy() to reduce memory
pressure.
</commits>

